    "input": "search_document: I understand your frustration regarding the missing discounts on your first order. Based on the handbook excerpts provided, here is the breakdown of the eligibility rules for the two specific benefits you mentioned:\n\n**1. Student Discount**\nThe handbook states that verified students are eligible for a permanent 10% discount on all regular-priced items. To activate this, customers must complete verification through a third-party partner service. Once verified, the discount is applied automatically to the account for 365 days [discounts]. The handbook does not contain information regarding troubleshooting why an automatic discount might fail to apply at checkout, nor does it provide a process for manually applying a discount to a completed order.\n\n**2. Referral Credit**\nThe handbook specifies that the **existing customer** (the referrer) earns a $20.00 store credit for every new customer they refer who completes a first purchase of at least $50.00. The **referred customer** (you) receives a **$10.00 discount** on their initial order, not a $20.00 credit [discounts]. Both parties must use the unique referral link provided in the email to qualify [discounts].\n\n**Conclusion and Next Steps**\n*   Regarding the **$20.00 credit**: The handbook indicates that the referred customer is eligible for a **$10.00 discount**, not a $20.00 credit. The $20.00 credit is awarded to the friend who referred you [discounts].\n*   Regarding the **10% student discount**: While you are eligible for this discount if verified, the handbook does not provide instructions for correcting a failed automatic application on a past order [discounts].\n\nSince the handbook excerpts do not cover the procedure for correcting a failed discount application or issuing credits for a completed order, I cannot provide the specific correction steps you requested. I recommend escalating this ticket to a human agent who can review your account verification status and the specific terms of your referral link usage to resolve the discrepancy.",
